FUTURE
main.cpp
--cpp26
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ a call satisfying the precondition: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
With the enforce evaluation semantic ([basic.contract.eval]/4: a contract
violation invokes the contract-violation handler, then the program is
terminated), as GCC 16 -fcontracts uses by default, f(0) violates its
precondition and the program ends there: the precondition is the one
property that fails.  CBMC parses pre() and post() but ignores them, so
verification succeeds.  Ignoring is one of the four evaluation semantics,
and which one is used is implementation-defined ([basic.contract.eval]/2),
so CBMC conforms; but a verifier is expected to check contracts.
goto-instrument's DFCC checks such contracts with --enforce-contract
(contracts-cpp-dfcc/cpp_pre_post).  Checked with GCC 16 -fcontracts (f(0)
aborts).
